{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"db3f5a0e-31a5-4090-bc59-8bdb75110087","step":{"id":"s1","script":"A standing wave forms when equal waves travel in opposite directions and interfere. Here, both ends of a stretched string are fixed.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Standing waves: fixed string"},{"op":"diagram","id":"string","region":"right","primitives":[{"kind":"line","points":[[0.1,0.5],[0.9,0.5]],"stroke":"dashed","meaning":"equilibrium position of the string"},{"kind":"line","points":[[0.1,0.35],[0.1,0.65]],"stroke":"solid","meaning":"fixed left support"},{"kind":"line","points":[[0.9,0.35],[0.9,0.65]],"stroke":"solid","meaning":"fixed right support"},{"kind":"smooth","points":[[0.1,0.5],[0.2,0.32],[0.3,0.25],[0.4,0.32],[0.5,0.5],[0.6,0.68],[0.7,0.75],[0.8,0.68],[0.9,0.5]],"stroke":"solid","meaning":"one instant of the second standing-wave mode"}],"tension":0.55}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"db3f5a0e-31a5-4090-bc59-8bdb75110087","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Nodes never move. Antinodes oscillate most strongly. This curve is one snapshot; the string does not travel along its curved shape.","ops":[{"op":"point","id":"node_left","canvas_id":"string","at":[0.1,0.5],"label":"node"},{"op":"point","id":"node_mid","canvas_id":"string","at":[0.5,0.5],"label":"node"},{"op":"point","id":"node_right","canvas_id":"string","at":[0.9,0.5],"label":"node"},{"op":"arrow","id":"oscillation","canvas_id":"string","from":[0.3,0.72],"to":[0.3,0.25],"stroke":"dashed","label":"antinode"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"At the middle node, does the string move up and down?","expected_gist":"No. A node stays at zero displacement."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"db3f5a0e-31a5-4090-bc59-8bdb75110087","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Neighboring nodes are half a wavelength apart. Two half-wavelength loops fit here, so this second mode has wavelength equal to the string length.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"length_rule","region":"A2","latex":"L=n\\frac{\\lambda_n}{2}"},{"op":"equation","id":"second_mode","anchor":{"el":"length_rule","side":"below","gap":0.06},"latex":"n=2:\\quad L=\\lambda_2"},{"op":"line","id":"length_mark","canvas_id":"string","from":[0.1,0.87],"to":[0.9,0.87],"stroke":"solid","label":"L"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"db3f5a0e-31a5-4090-bc59-8bdb75110087","step":{"id":"s4","script":"Fixed ends allow only whole numbers of half-wavelengths. Using wave speed equals frequency times wavelength gives the harmonic frequencies: integer multiples of the fundamental.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"speed_rule","anchor":{"el":"second_mode","side":"below","gap":0.07},"latex":"v=f_n\\lambda_n"},{"op":"equation","id":"harmonics","anchor":{"el":"speed_rule","side":"below","gap":0.06},"latex":"f_n=\\frac{nv}{2L}=nf_1,\\quad n=1,2,3,\\ldots"}],"checkpoint":null}}
